  "id": "Compound::DB00367 - Gene::2099",
  "verbalization": "Levonorgestrel is implicated in the activation of Esr1 through a series of interconnected pathways involving progestins and hormonal signaling. The evidence suggests that Levonorgestrel, through its progestin activity, leads to the production of genital discharge and premenstrual tension, both of which are mediated by Etonogestrel, a related progestin. Etonogestrel then binds to Esr1, indicating a direct interaction that may contribute to the physiological effects observed. The shared lowest common ancestors, 17Beta-Hydroxy Steroid and Progestin, further support a common biochemical pathway involving progestin receptor activation. While the exact mechanism of Esr1 activation by Levonorgestrel remains partially elucidated, the evidence points to a progestin-mediated signaling cascade that ultimately involves Esr1. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of progestins in modulating hormonal responses and their potential impact on receptor activation.",
